  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1207 PM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\n\nThis Monday morning an upper level ridge was located over the \nwestern United States with a northwest early flow aloft across \nthe Plains. A surface ridge extended from southwest Kansas into \nIowa a a trough of low pressure at the surface develops along \nthe lee of the Rockies. Models are in good agreement today with \nthe upper ridge remaining over the western United States early \nin the week as a weak cold front drops south into the Central \nPlains Tuesday night. This cold front will lift back north \ntowards Nebraska on Wednesday as the west to northwest flow \naloft continues across western Kansas. During the last half of \nthe work week the upper level ridge will move east and weaken as\nan upper level trough exits the eastern Pacific and begins to \ncross the Pacific Northwest. Given the persistent northwest to \nwesterly flow and upper ridge moving east and weakening through \nthe start of the weekend period the temperatures across \nsouthwest Kansas will be near or slightly above the seasonal \nnorms for early October with no precipitation expected despite \nthe weak cold front mid week. This period of warm and dry \nconditions however will be ending by the beginning of next week \nas the upper level trough over the Pacific northwest approaches \nfrom the west late this weekend/early next week will deepen and \nmove east across the western United States. \n\nEarly Autumn warmth expected across southwest Kansas through \nthe weekend period. 850mb temperatures will warm into the 20 to \naround 25C range on Tuesday ahead of an approaching cold front \nwhich will allow temperatures to warm well into the 80s on \nTuesday. The cold front will move into southwest Kansas and \nstall out Tuesday night before lifting back north Wednesday. \nSouth of this boundary the temperatures on Wednesday will still \nbe unseasonably warm (Highs in the low to mid 80s) while north \nof this boundary the high temperatures will end up being more \nseasonal (Highs around 80). What cooler, more seasonal, air that\ndoes filter into southwest Kansas on Wednesday will erode from \nsouth to north as this cold front lifts north back into \nnorthern Kansas as a west to northwest downslope flow continues \nacross western Kansas. Given this downslope flow and the \nforecast 850mb temperatures each afternoon late week the high \ntemperatures are not expected to vary much from earlier in the \nweek. Highs at least in the 80 to 85 degree range late week.\n\nLooking ahead to next week, we are currently monitoring the \nchances for precipitation early next week along with some cooler\ntemperatures. Models this morning appear to be struggling with \nthe timing and evolution of our next, significant, upper level \nsystem as it moves across the western Untied States and deepens.\nForecast confidence from Sunday through early next week is low \n(<30%) but at this time based on the latest ensemble trends the \nwesterly downslope flow late week across the Rockies will back \nto the southwest by late this weekend. As this occurs the chance\nfor precipitation will begin to improve as out next cold front \ndrops south across the Northern Plains. The combination of the \napproaching upper level system along with the cold front \ncrossing southwest Kansas will result in improving chances for \nprecipitation. When this will actually occur however is unclear \nso will stay close to the latest forecast with rain chances \nimproving on Monday as cooler air returns with the cold frontal \npassage.",
